---
/**
 * The idea the whole site started from: stack many ideas that each have a modest chance
 * of beating the market, and beat it. The honest answer, in plain words and no formulas.
 * Every number here is arithmetic on a measured correlation or a count from the research.
 */
import Button from "~/components/Button.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import StatGrid from "~/components/StatGrid.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

const STATS = [
  { value: "55%", label: "One idea's chance of beating the market", note: "The assumption the thesis starts from" },
  {
    value: "58%",
    label: "The ceiling, however many you add",
    note: "When the ideas move together as much as this portfolio's leans do, 0.43 out of 1",
  },
  { value: "3 or 4", label: "Truly separate bets available", note: "Stocks, cheap companies, trend, bonds" },
  { value: "30 years", label: "To know an edge of about 0.7% a year is real", note: "Smaller edges take longer" },
];
---

<Base
  title="Stacking many small edges"
  description="If you stack many ideas that each have a 55% chance of beating the market, you should beat it. The arithmetic is right if the ideas are unrelated; the ideas an ordinary investor can buy move together, so stacking tops out near a 58% chance however many you add."
  xl
>
  <Hero
    title="Can you beat the market by stacking many small edges?"
    lede="If you stack many ideas that each have a 55% chance of beating the market, you should beat it. That is the thesis this site was built to test, and the answer is yes on paper and no in the shop."
  >
    <p class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-ink-2">
      <Verdict word="Settled" gloss="The ceiling is arithmetic on a correlation measured over 422 months." />
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <StatGrid items={STATS} class="wide mt-10" />

  <div class="prose mt-10">
    <h2 id="the-arithmetic">The arithmetic is right</h2>
    <p>Take an idea with a 55% chance of beating a plain world stock index in a given year: a coin with a slight
    bend. Stack several such ideas that have nothing to do with each other and the odds improve, because their
    bad years do not line up. With perfectly unrelated ideas you would need five for a 60% chance, 18 for 70%,
    45 for 80%, 105 for 90% and 172 for 95%. "Stack a ton" is true at roughly 170 to 340 unrelated bets, which
    describes a market maker with a floor of traders. It does not describe eight funds.</p>

    <h2 id="why-it-stops">Why it stops working</h2>
    <p>The ideas an ordinary investor can buy are related. The three leans in the value-lean portfolio, toward
    cheap US companies, cheap small foreign companies and cheap emerging-market companies, move together at
    about 0.43 out of 1, measured over 422 months. Stack an unlimited number of 55% ideas that are related to
    that degree and the chance of beating the market climbs to 57.6% and stops. Not 90%, and not 100%. Five
    such ideas capture two thirds of everything an infinite pile could deliver, ten capture four fifths, and the
    hundredth adds nothing.</p>
    <p>A second way to see it: twenty genuinely unrelated bets of that size, with no fees, still lose to the
    index 29 years in a hundred, and given a modest relationship to each other, 0.2 out of 1, they are worth
    about four bets with 60% odds.</p>

    <h2 id="how-many-bets">How many separate bets exist</h2>
    <p>Counted honestly, about four or five genuinely distinct sources of return exist for a US investor buying
    funds: stocks, a lean toward cheap companies, trend following, bonds, and perhaps one more. In a typical
    eight-holding institutional portfolio, stocks alone are 81% of the risk. The eight funds in the Plus trend
    portfolio are five active positions worth about 3.7 separate bets, and in the worst tenth of stock months
    that falls to about 2.8, because the three cheap-company leans merge in a crisis. They merge; they do not
    fail together.</p>
    <p>Geography adds little: one idea spread across three regions is worth about one and a half bets out of
    three, because the regions fall together. Style adds more: five different ideas inside one country count as
    five and a half bets, because cheapness and rising prices move opposite ways. Hold
    <a href="/strategies/international/">foreign stocks</a> for their price, and not for diversification.</p>
    <p>The best real-world test of stacking is a multi-strategy fund run by the people who invented the
    approach. Over 2013 to 2026 it delivered about a quarter of its forecast, returning 7.6% a year against
    about 13.5% for the S&amp;P 500, with genuine independence from stocks. Done well, the honest prize is a
    manager's forecast of about 1.5% to 2% a year against what an investor would otherwise hold, and never a
    claim to beat the index.</p>

    <h2 id="the-four-portfolios">What this means for the four portfolios</h2>
    <p>How you pay for an idea decides whether it adds. When you buy an idea by selling part of your portfolio,
    the portfolio's edge is a weighted average of its ideas and can never beat the best one. Only an idea added
    on top, with money borrowed inside a fund, adds its edge as a sum. In the Plus trend portfolio only the
    trend fund is added on top; the value leans are bought by selling other stocks. Making a weight smaller
    does not change the odds, only the size of the prize.</p>
    <p>Against a cheap index fund, the whole edge the research could find comes to about 0.05% a year against
    3.1% a year of wobble: a 54% chance of being ahead after thirty years, a coin flip, which is why
    <a href="/portfolios/one-fund/">the one-fund portfolio</a> is the default. The one edge the data can
    resolve, about 0.8% a year from the cheap-company leans, is worth over a career about the same as saving an
    extra 3% of what you started with every year. The <a href="/portfolios/value-lean/">value-lean
    portfolio</a> takes that bet; <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">Plus trend</a> adds the one idea that is
    financed on top.</p>

    <h2 id="how-long">How long it takes to know</h2>
    <p>Thirty years of holding can demonstrate an edge of about 0.73% a year and nothing smaller. An idea whose
    edge cannot be shown positive does not become provable with patience; thirty years turns an unreadable edge
    into a longer wait for the same coin flip.</p>

    <h2 id="more-funds">Why "stack more funds" stops helping</h2>
    <p>Offered twelve candidate ideas, the best buy-only portfolio holds three and does not improve past three.
    Most of the benefit of more ideas lives in betting against some of them, which a buy-only investor cannot
    reach. Every fund added past that point brings its fee for certain and its edge as a maybe.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
    <Button href="/portfolios/">Compare the four portfolios</Button>
    <Button href="/strategies/" variant="outline">Every other idea</Button>
  </div>
</Base>
